  "type": "singleCorrect",
  "level": 1,
  "question": {
    "text": "The alkali metals form salt-like hydrides by the direct synthesis at elevated temperature. The thermal stability of these hydrides decreases in which of the following orders?",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "v3Fzl",
      "text": "$\\mathrm{CsH}>\\mathrm{RbH}>\\mathrm{KH}>\\mathrm{NaH}>\\mathrm{LiH}$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "avabx",
      "text": "$\\mathrm{KH}>\\mathrm{NaH}>\\mathrm{LiH}>\\mathrm{CsH}>\\mathrm{RbH}$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "ZLeDv",
      "text": "$\\mathrm{NaH}>\\mathrm{LiH}>\\mathrm{KH}>\\mathrm{RbH}>\\mathrm{CsH}$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "b3PV5",
      "text": "$\\mathrm{LiH}>\\mathrm{NaH}>\\mathrm{KH}>\\mathrm{RbH}>\\mathrm{CsH}$",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "The stability of alkali metal hydrides decreases from Li to Cs. It is due to the fact that $\\mathrm{M}-\\mathrm{H}$ bonds becomes weaker with increase in size of alkali metals as we move down the group from $\\mathrm{Li}$ to $\\mathrm{Cs}$. Thus the order of stability of hydrides is $\\mathrm{LiH}>\\mathrm{NaH}>\\mathrm{KH}>\\mathrm{RbH}>\\mathrm{CsH}$",
    "image": null
  },
